{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015bXCS7UQ3iQemGJ5jetZfb","type":"tool_result","content":"# Key Study Details\n\n**(1) Number of Students:**\n194 students participated. \"Of the 233 enrolled students, 194 were eligible for inclusion in the study.\"\n\n**(2) Design:**\nYes, crossover design. \"The present study was approved by the Harvard University IRB (study no. IRB23-0797) and followed a crossover design.\"\n\n**(3) Effect Size:**\nStandard linear regression showed 0.63 SD. \"While the linear regression suggests an effect size of 0.63, this is an underestimation due to ceiling effect; a quantile regression allows us to provide an estimate of the effect size that avoids ceiling effect in the post-test scores. Such an analysis provides an effect size in the range of 0.73 to 1.3 standard deviations.\"\n\n**(4) Median Time on Task:**\nAI group: 49 minutes; in-class group: 60 minutes. \"For students in the AI group, we tracked their use of the AI tutor platform to measure how long they spent on the material... The median time on task for students in the AI group was 49 minutes\" and \"During a 75-minute period, the in-class students spent 15 minutes taking the pre- and post-tests; we assume 60 minutes spent on learning.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d12dfc6-d7ef-4d13-8531-39e6848a761b","uuid":"aecc881a-a865-4bf4-8338-3978b88396a2","timestamp":"2026-08-10T23:45:39.033Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":345258,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Key Study Details\n\n**(1) Number of Students:**\n194 students participated. \"Of the 233 enrolled students, 194 were eligible for inclusion in the study.\"\n\n**(2) Design:**\nYes, crossover design. \"The present study was approved by the Harvard University IRB (study no. IRB23-0797) and followed a crossover design.\"\n\n**(3) Effect Size:**\nStandard linear regression showed 0.63 SD. \"While the linear regression suggests an effect size of 0.63, this is an underestimation due to ceiling effect; a quantile regression allows us to provide an estimate of the effect size that avoids ceiling effect in the post-test scores. Such an analysis provides an effect size in the range of 0.73 to 1.3 standard deviations.\"\n\n**(4) Median Time on Task:**\nAI group: 49 minutes; in-class group: 60 minutes. \"For students in the AI group, we tracked their use of the AI tutor platform to measure how long they spent on the material... The median time on task for students in the AI group was 49 minutes\" and \"During a 75-minute period, the in-class students spent 15 minutes taking the pre- and post-tests; we assume 60 minutes spent on learning.\"","durationMs":5903,"url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-97652-6?error=cookies_not_supported&code=7084e863-f5f5-4615-b0d1-02d91fe308b1"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0135jXpYtMNcvMJtAPDS3jGc","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"De Simone \"From Chalkboards to Chatbots\" Nigeria World Bank Policy Research Working Paper 11125 effect size\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ERIC - ED676624 - From Chalkboards to Chatbots: Evaluating the Impact of Generative AI on Learning Outcomes in Nigeria. Policy Research Working Paper 11125, World Bank, 2025-May\",\"url\":\"https://eric.ed.gov/?q=source:%22World+Bank%22&id=ED676624\"},{\"title\":\"Nigeria - Reproducibility package for From Chalkboards To Chatbots: Evaluating The Impact Of Generative Ai On Learning Outcomes In Nigeria\",\"url\":\"https://reproducibility.worldbank.org/catalog/419/study-description\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluating Gemini in an arena for learning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.24477\"},{\"title\":\"From Chalkboards to Chatbots : Evaluating the Impact of Generative AI on Learning Outcomes in Nigeria\",\"url\":\"https://ideas.repec.org/p/wbk/wbrwps/11125.html\"},{\"title\":\"From Chalkboards to Chatbots: How Generative AI is Reshaping Classroom Learning in Nigeria — AITrends.ng\",\"url\":\"https://www.aitrends.ng/post/from-chalkboards-to-chatbots-how-generative-ai-is-reshaping-classroom-learning\"},{\"title\":\"Teacher Professional Development on WhatsApp and LLMs: Early Lessons from Cameroon\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.04139\"},{\"title\":\"From Chalkboards to Chatbots : Evaluating the Impact of Generative AI on Learning Outcomes in Nigeria\",\"url\":\"https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099548105192529324\"},{\"title\":\"openknowledge.worldbank.org\",\"url\":\"https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/bitstreams/cd9cca5b-2ffd-4be1-8313-0f4d2ed6ef4f/download\"},{\"title\":\"VideoFebruary 18, 2025\",\"url\":\"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/video/2025/02/18/ai-digitaldevelopment-from-chalkboards-to-chatbots\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are the key effect sizes from the De Simone et al. paper \"From Chalkboards to Chatbots\" (World Bank Policy Research Working Paper 11125):\n\nThe intervention demonstrated a significant improvement of 0.31 standard deviations on an assessment that included English topics aligned with the Nigerian curriculum, knowledge of artificial intelligence and digital skills. More specifically, the effect on English, the main outcome of interest, was of 0.23 standard deviations.\n\nAdditionally, an analysis of heterogeneous effects shows that while the program benefits students across the baseline ability distribution, the largest effects are for female students, and those with higher initial academic performance. Cost-effectiveness analysis revealed substantial learning gains, equating to 1.5 to 2 years of 'business-as-usual' schooling, situating the intervention among some of the most cost-effective programs to improve learning outcomes.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d12dfc6-d7ef-4d13-8531-39e6848a761b","uuid":"053a3086-6012-4519-9003-83957c70ff39","timestamp":"2026-08-10T23:45:40.016Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"De Simone \"From Chalkboards to Chatbots\" Nigeria World Bank Policy Research Working Paper 11125 effect size","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GwvKTyXwJs6KJg4KMucV1g","content":[{"title":"ERIC - ED676624 - From Chalkboards to Chatbots: Evaluating the Impact of Generative AI on Learning Outcomes in Nigeria. Policy Research Working Paper 11125, World Bank, 2025-May","url":"https://eric.ed.gov/?q=source:%22World+Bank%22&id=ED676624"},{"title":"Nigeria - Reproducibility package for From Chalkboards To Chatbots: Evaluating The Impact Of Generative Ai On Learning Outcomes In Nigeria","url":"https://reproducibility.worldbank.org/catalog/419/study-description"},{"title":"Evaluating Gemini in an arena for learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.24477"},{"title":"From Chalkboards to Chatbots : Evaluating the Impact of Generative AI on Learning Outcomes in Nigeria","url":"https://ideas.repec.org/p/wbk/wbrwps/11125.html"},{"title":"From Chalkboards to Chatbots: How Generative AI is Reshaping Classroom Learning in Nigeria — AITrends.ng","url":"https://www.aitrends.ng/post/from-chalkboards-to-chatbots-how-generative-ai-is-reshaping-classroom-learning"},{"title":"Teacher Professional Development on WhatsApp and LLMs: Early Lessons from Cameroon","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.04139"},{"title":"From Chalkboards to Chatbots : Evaluating the Impact of Generative AI on Learning Outcomes in Nigeria","url":"https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099548105192529324"},{"title":"openknowledge.worldbank.org","url":"https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/bitstreams/cd9cca5b-2ffd-4be1-8313-0f4d2ed6ef4f/download"},{"title":"VideoFebruary 18, 2025","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/video/2025/02/18/ai-digitaldevelopment-from-chalkboards-to-chatbots"}]},"Based on the search results, here are the key effect sizes from the De Simone et al. paper \"From Chalkboards to Chatbots\" (World Bank Policy Research Working Paper 11125):\n\nThe intervention demonstrated a significant improvement of 0.31 standard deviations on an assessment that included English topics aligned with the Nigerian curriculum, knowledge of artificial intelligence and digital skills. More specifically, the effect on English, the main outcome of interest, was of 0.23 standard deviations.\n\nAdditionally, an analysis of heterogeneous effects shows that while the program benefits students across the baseline ability distribution, the largest effects are for female students, and those with higher initial academic performance. Cost-effectiveness analysis revealed substantial learning gains, equating to 1.5 to 2 years of 'business-as-usual' schooling, situating the intervention among some of the mos